{"id":"760dc35f-7e6b-4074-83cd-24a7961c9600","object":"chat.completion","created":1793335690,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"\n\n```python\nprint(read_range(\"deepinfer/environ.py\", 1, 30)[\"content\"])\nprint(\"----LOGGER HEAD----\")\nprint(read_range(\"deepinfer/logger.py\", 1, 30)[\"content\"])\nprint(\"----STATS----\")\nprint(search_code(\"logger.info\", [\"deepinfer/engine/stats_logger.py\"], 20))\nprint(\"----TESTLOGGER----\")\nprint(read_file(\".course/test_logger.py\")[\"content\"][:6000])\n```\n\n```python\nprint(read_file(\".course/test_logging_config.py\")[\"content\"][:6000])\n```","reasoning_content":"We need to fix the logger.py implementation. Currently NewLineFormatter has not been extended with max_message_chars, and _setup_logger doesn't pass the config. Also duplicate DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS in environ.py — we should remove one (keep in Logging section, remove the earlier one). Also empty string handling: int(\"\") raises ValueError, fine. Error text must contain variable name — descriptor wraps with name prefix, good.\n\nNeed to implement truncation logic:\n- budget B = max_message_chars\n- message after arg substitution = record.message (computed by super().format(record) — actually record.message set by Formatter.format via record.getMessage()).\n- marker = \"...<omitted>...\" length 15.\n- If len(msg) > B: remaining = B - 15. head = ceil(remaining/2), tail = remaining - head. truncated = msg[:head] + marker + msg[-tail:] (if tail == 0, msg[-0:] is whole string — careful! Use msg[len(msg)-tail:] when tail>0 else \"\").\n- Keep prefix/exception handling: super().format includes prefix and exception text. We should truncate only the message body while keeping prefix. Approach: compute full = super().format(record); the prefix goes up to record.message occurrence. Better: compute truncated message, then build output by replacing record.message in full with truncated message? But record.message may appear elsewhere. Alternative: use self._style.format(record)? \n\nCleaner: since format() calls super().format(record), we can compute the message first: message = record.getMessage(); then temporarily set record.message? Actually the standard: Formatter.format computes record.message = record.getMessage(), then s = self.formatMessage(record). So we can override formatMessage to truncate record.message? No—formatMessage uses self._style.format(record) with record.message, and record.message is used for %(message)s. Truncating record.message mutation would affect shared record... but record.message gets overwritten anyway by any formatter; but requirement says don't rewrite msg/args/exc_info/stack_info — record.message isn't in that list, but safer to not mutate.\n\nApproach: in format(), do:\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    truncated = self._truncate(message)  # based on len\n    if truncated is not message:\n        # temporarily substitute\n        ...\n```\nSimplest: set record.message to truncated before calling super().format()? super().format(record) calls record.getMessage() again and assigns record.message = that. It will overwrite. So instead call self.formatMessage with a shallow copy? Then prefix from fmt uses record attributes — a shallow copy via copy.copy(record) would work but need copy import; but \"可增加必要标准库导入\". Alternatively:\n\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    msg = super().format(record)   # full text with prefix and exception\n    full_message = record.message\n    if budget is not None and not keep_only_true and len(full_message) > budget:\n        truncated = truncate(full_message)\n        # replace only the first occurrence of full_message in msg\n        idx = msg.find(full_message)\n        ... but prefix msecs might contain same text; use idx from the message position: the message appears after prefix, and exception text after.\n```\nActually the original code does `prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]`. So they assume find works. We can do: after super().format(record), find the message in msg and replace with truncated version:\n```\ntruncated = ...\nif truncated is not None:\n    idx = msg.find(full_message)\n    if idx >= 0:\n        msg = msg[:idx] + truncated + msg[idx+len(full_message):]\n```\nEdge: full_message might be empty → no truncation anyway.\n\nBut careful: if the message contains newlines and we need prefix alignment; existing code aligns after. That's fine.\n\nAlternatively, a cleaner way: build the record copy. Let's consider tests: test_message_budget_and_alignment with multiline; test checks output content presumably. Using replacement approach should be fine.\n\nHmm, but a subtlety: exception text appended after message — when truncating we keep the message body truncated and exception intact. With the replace approach, exception stays. Good.\n\nAnother subtlety: whats if record.message appears in the time prefix? e.g. message \"2024\". find gives the prefix occurrence, causing broken output. Safer: compute prefix length by formatting? We can get the prefix via msg.find(record.message) like original... same risk. Better approach: compute the head portion deterministically.\n\nBetter solution: override formatMessage? Actually we can compute the prefix ourselves:\nprefix = self._style.format(record) which produces the prefix + message using record.message. Then prefix_str = full_style[: len(full_style) - len(record.message)] if it endswith record.message. formatMessage returns self._style.format(record) i.e. prefix+message. So:\n\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    truncated = self._apply_budget(record, message)\n    if truncated is not None:\n        # temporarily set record.message\n        original = record.message\n        record.message = truncated\n        try:\n            result = super().format(record)\n        finally:\n            record.message = original\n    else:\n        result = super().format(record)\n```\nBut super().format sets record.message = record.getMessage() at the start, ignoring our assignment. So that won't work.\n\nUse copy.copy: \n```\nimport copy\nrecord2 = copy.copy(record)\nrecord2.message = truncated\n```\nBut record.getMessage() would recompute from args if... no, copy retains args; super().format calls record.getMessage() again and overwrites message. Damn.\n\nSo instead: build manually. Replicate Formatter.format:\n```\ns = self.formatMessage(record)  # uses record.message -> full\n```\nThen we can compute prefix by stripping the message from end: formatMessage returns \"%(prefix)s\" + message, so if s.endswith(message): prefix = s[:len(s)-len(message)]. Then with truncated, formatMessage would produce prefix + truncated. But exception text (formatException) is appended by base format after formatMessage.\n\nCleanest robust approach: implement our own formatMessage subclass semantics:\n\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    truncated = self._truncate(record, message)\n    # Let base class format everything; then substitute prefix-safe\n```\nAlternative: temporarily monkeypatch record.getMessage to return truncated message! Then super().format(record) computes record.message = truncated_message and everything works naturally, and exception appended normally.\n\n```\noriginal_get_message = record.getMessage\nrecord.getMessage = lambda: truncated\ntry:\n    return self._format_full(record)  # does super().format\nfinally:\n    record.getMessage = original\n```\nBut record is a LogRecord with __dict__; assigning instance attribute getMessage is allowed (LogRecord isn't slotted). However mutating shared record object temporarily - it's restored. Hmm risky but effective. But also record.message set to truncated remains on record after, which affects subsequent handlers: requirement says same record handed to full handler must retain full message. Handlers call formatter.format(record) which recomputes record.message from getMessage anyway (base format always sets record.message = record.getMessage()). So fine. But safer to save and restore record.message too.\n\nActually maybe simpler: compute the formatted prefix ourselves using self._style directly? Hmm, formatMessage = self._style.format(record) which uses record.message. We can do:\n\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    ...\n```\nThen replicate logging.Formatter.format body:\n\n```\nrecord.message = truncated   # temporarily\ntry:\n    s = self.formatMessage(record)\n    if record.exc_info:\n        if not record.exc_text:\n            record.exc_text = self.formatException(record.exc_info)\n    if record.exc_text:\n        if s[-1:] != \"\\n\":\n            s = s + \"\\n\"\n        s = s + record.exc_text\n    if record.stack_info:\n        if s[-1:] != \"\\n\":\n            s = s + \"\\n\"\n        s = s + self.formatStack(record.stack_info)\nfinally:\n    record.message = message\n```\nThat's replicating. Risk of Python version differences (3.11 vs 3.12: stack_info handling similar). Acceptable? Hmm, a bit brittle.\n\nThe monkeypatch approach: \n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    if budget is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n        return super().format(record)\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    if len(message) <= budget:\n        return super().format(record)\n    truncated = _truncate(message, budget)\n    ...\n```\nWait requirement 7: `keep_full_message` exactly True → full. For other records with budget set and message too long, truncate.\n\nThen use monkeypatch:\n```\n    original_get_message = record.getMessage\n    original_message = record.message  # hmm could raise AttributeError before base format\n```\nWe can avoid touching record at all by using a lightweight proxy? super().format(record) accesses many attrs of record: levelname, name, etc., plus getMessage. A proxy class delegating via __getattr__ and overriding getMessage would work:\n\nActually simpler: use copy.copy(record) and then override getMessage by setting instance attribute on the copy:\n```\nimport copy\nclone = copy.copy(record)\nclone.getMessage = lambda: truncated\n```\nBut base format does `record.message = record.getMessage()` — works on clone. And the clone shares exc_info, stack_info. And record itself never modified. \n\nBut wait: in copy, `record.message` may carry over from prior formatting? Base format recomputes anyway. Good.\n\n`copy` is a standard import; allowed? \"可增加必要标准库导入\" for logger.py. copy is standard. Might use `logging.makeLogRecord` instead? Not needed.\n\nHowever, careful: logging.Formatter.format for Python 3.11+:\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    record.message = record.getMessage()\n    if self.usesTime():\n        record.asctime = self.formatTime(record, self.datefmt)\n    s = self.formatMessage(record)\n    ...\n```\nOur formatTime override uses record.created/msecs — clone shares them. Fine.\n\nAlso our format overrides uses record.message in the multiline alignment after. With clone approach, we call super().format(clone) → clone.message = truncated. Then alignment check uses record.message; but on the clone... we use `msg` and `record.message`. Let's restructure:\n\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        if len(message) > self.max_message_chars:\n            record = copy.copy(record)\n            record.getMessage = ...  # lambda returning truncated\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    ...\n```\nBut then after super().format, `record.message` on the clone is the truncated one; the alignment code does `msg.find(record.message)` — msg contains prefix+truncated message (+exception). find of truncated body works. Hmm, but careful: if prefix contains the truncated text... negligible.\n\nActually to be safe for the alignment use: original code uses msg.find(record.message) to get prefix. Keep as is.\n\nNow, does cloning break the color? No.\n\nInstances: `record.getMessage = lambda: truncated` — but LogRecord instances allow attribute assignment. Yes (LogRecord has __dict__).\n\nAlternatively define a helper function:\n```\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, budget: int) -> str:\n    marker = \"...<omitted>...\"\n    remaining = budget - len(marker)\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2   # ceil\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + marker + (message[len(message)-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\nNote requirement: \"剩余空间为预算减去标记长度\" — with budget 32, marker 15, remaining 17, head ceil(17/2)=9, tail 8. ✓ matches example.\n\nAlso guard budget >= len(marker)? budget>=32 > 15, fine.\n\nValidation in __init__:\n```\ndef __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\n    super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n    self.high_precision = high_precision\n    self.use_color = use_color\n    if max_message_chars is not None:\n        if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n            raise ValueError(...)\n        if max_message_chars < 32:\n            raise ValueError(...)\n    self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\nError message text: what do tests expect? Probably matching \"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS\"? Let's check the tests in .course/test_logger.py to know expected messages. Let's read the tests (allowed to read, not modify).\n\nSetup in _setup_logger: `max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. Careful: accessing envs attribute inside _setup_logger at import time could raise ValueError if env var invalid at import — but tests set it before import presumably. Also requirement: re-running default init applies current config; accessing triggers parse first time.\n\nBut circular import: logger imports environ? Check environ imports logger? Need to check. environ.py imports? It imports os, threading, timedelta... probably fine. But logger imports environ → environ imports nothing from logger. Let's check environ top lines.\n\nAlso duplicate env var definitions must be removed (the one at line ~117 in Runtime section). Let's read files properly and check test expectations.\n\nAlso note changing the earlier already-defined attribute may matter. Remove the first duplicate.\n\nLet's inspect environ.py head and full logger file, plus tests."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":8590,"completion_tokens":3137,"total_tokens":11727,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3003},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":4494},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}